36
00:01:40,260 --> 00:01:46,600
 All right Sam, so your contribution

37
00:01:46,600 --> 00:01:50,360
 to this AI and crypto's use cases piece

38
00:01:50,360 --> 00:01:53,280
 was that agents should have universal identity.

39
00:01:53,280 --> 00:01:54,840
 What do you mean by that?

40
00:01:54,840 --> 00:01:58,720
 I think that when you go to a traditional application,

41
00:01:58,720 --> 00:02:01,400
 you go to the website or you go to the app on your phone,

42
00:02:01,400 --> 00:02:04,280
 you log in and you see your stuff there.

43
00:02:04,280 --> 00:02:07,120
 But what happens when you're working with an agent

44
00:02:07,120 --> 00:02:09,200
 that might show up in its own app,

45
00:02:09,200 --> 00:02:14,200
 but also on Slack or on a phone call over email, over text,

46
00:02:14,200 --> 00:02:17,280
 it gets a lot harder to know when you're interacting

47
00:02:17,280 --> 00:02:18,840
 with an agent with the right context

48
00:02:18,840 --> 00:02:21,760
 and right capabilities that you're authorized against.

49
00:02:21,760 --> 00:02:24,080
 And so a universal identity for agents

50
00:02:24,080 --> 00:02:25,320
 is a way of expressing this.

51
00:02:25,320 --> 00:02:28,280
 Like a unique, uniform resource identifier,

52
00:02:28,280 --> 00:02:29,440
 but it's not really rich enough.

53
00:02:29,440 --> 00:02:31,160
 And so like, how do you do payments?

54
00:02:31,160 --> 00:02:32,280
 How do you list capabilities?

55
00:02:32,280 --> 00:02:33,560
 How do you share a state?

56
00:02:33,560 --> 00:02:35,680
 And so we start to make this point

57
00:02:35,680 --> 00:02:37,240
 in longer strokes in that piece.

58
00:02:37,240 --> 00:02:40,280
 So talk to me, talk to me about why this is important.

59
00:02:40,280 --> 00:02:41,840
 Why does it matter that an agent

60
00:02:41,840 --> 00:02:43,920
 should have an identity of its own?

61
00:02:43,920 --> 00:02:48,600
 Agent state is not quite like traditional app state.

62
00:02:48,600 --> 00:02:52,600
 You wanna talk to a certain model, a certain LLM,

63
00:02:52,600 --> 00:02:54,120
 you wanna talk to a certain context,

64
00:02:54,120 --> 00:02:55,320
 what does the agent know about you?

65
00:02:55,320 --> 00:02:56,760
 You wanna talk to a certain set of capabilities.

66
00:02:56,760 --> 00:02:58,520
 And all that wrapped together

67
00:02:58,520 --> 00:03:00,520
 is what we think of as an agent.

68
00:03:00,520 --> 00:03:04,440
 And I think that to connect with an agent like that,

69
00:03:04,440 --> 00:03:07,200
 you're gonna need to be able to identify in a way

70
00:03:07,200 --> 00:03:09,800
 that is richer than with websites.

71
00:03:09,800 --> 00:03:11,840
 Let me give you an example.

72
00:03:11,840 --> 00:03:14,560
 So if you wanna book a hotel today,

73
00:03:14,560 --> 00:03:18,280
 you might go to a kayak.com or some other aggregator.

74
00:03:18,280 --> 00:03:19,560
 But what if you wanna book a whole trip

75
00:03:19,560 --> 00:03:20,880
 and you wanna do it with an agent?

76
00:03:20,880 --> 00:03:22,980
 Well, you might text the agent and say,

77
00:03:22,980 --> 00:03:24,760
 find me a nice hotel in Paris.

78
00:03:24,760 --> 00:03:27,160
 You might go to the agent's website

79
00:03:27,160 --> 00:03:29,440
 to look at pictures of the agent

80
00:03:29,440 --> 00:03:31,640
 and you might call the agent.

81
00:03:31,640 --> 00:03:34,440
 And for each of these three interactions,

82
00:03:34,440 --> 00:03:36,320
 ideally you're talking to the same agent

83
00:03:36,320 --> 00:03:39,120
 that remembers all of the rest of the context before.

84
00:03:39,120 --> 00:03:41,000
 You could even imagine a situation where you're

85
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:44,680
 in another app, like you're maybe on your workplace slack,

86
00:03:44,680 --> 00:03:47,640
 maybe you're sitting in a third party application

87
00:03:47,640 --> 00:03:49,760
 looking at like food recommendations

88
00:03:49,760 --> 00:03:52,880
 and you wanna bring that same agent into the context.

89
00:03:52,880 --> 00:03:54,120
 That's a really rich interaction

90
00:03:54,120 --> 00:03:56,200
 and you need a way of universally identifying

91
00:03:56,200 --> 00:03:58,440
 and then locating that agent

92
00:03:58,440 --> 00:04:01,080
 so you can bring it into whatever context you're working in

93
00:04:01,080 --> 00:04:03,240
 even though you talk to it in multiple places.

94
00:04:03,240 --> 00:04:05,520
 So just to make this really concrete for people,

95
00:04:05,520 --> 00:04:08,360
 you're giving this example of booking a hotel,

96
00:04:08,360 --> 00:04:12,000
 booking a trip, but you're literally in Paris right now

97
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:13,880
 sitting in a hotel room that you booked.

98
00:04:13,880 --> 00:04:15,280
 And before we got on this call,

99
00:04:15,280 --> 00:04:16,560
 you were talking to our producer

100
00:04:16,560 --> 00:04:19,440
 about food recommendations in the city.

101
00:04:19,440 --> 00:04:21,480
 So this is a very real problem

102
00:04:21,480 --> 00:04:23,720
 that you are navigating right now.

103
00:04:23,720 --> 00:04:25,920
 - Okay, well, I wanna go on to get a nice dinner

104
00:04:25,920 --> 00:04:27,920
 with a good friend tomorrow night.

105
00:04:27,920 --> 00:04:30,120
 But I will say, like I have the good fortune

106
00:04:30,120 --> 00:04:31,960
 to have a person that I can talk to

107
00:04:31,960 --> 00:04:33,760
 to help me book this restaurant.

108
00:04:33,760 --> 00:04:35,560
 And I'll tell you what my experience is with them.

109
00:04:35,560 --> 00:04:36,640
 I'm sending them links.

110
00:04:36,640 --> 00:04:38,520
 I've talked to them on the phone.

111
00:04:38,520 --> 00:04:40,200
 I've talked to them in person, obviously.

112
00:04:40,200 --> 00:04:42,320
 And I've got, you know, I've got Google's reservation window

113
00:04:42,320 --> 00:04:43,920
 that I can look at.

114
00:04:43,920 --> 00:04:47,000
 But I'm sort of keeping track of all this information myself

115
00:04:47,000 --> 00:04:49,320
 and the person that I work with, this assistant,

116
00:04:49,320 --> 00:04:51,960
 you know, I know all the places I can contact them.

117
00:04:51,960 --> 00:04:54,680
 And for our agents, we probably need a similar thing.

118
00:04:54,680 --> 00:04:57,600
 We need a way of contacting them on any platform

119
00:04:57,600 --> 00:04:59,520
 and sharing contacts with them

120
00:04:59,520 --> 00:05:02,440
 while understanding their capabilities.

121
00:05:02,440 --> 00:05:04,640
 And so for friends, I know their capabilities are like

122
00:05:04,640 --> 00:05:07,640
 when they're awake and sort of if they're by their laptop

123
00:05:07,640 --> 00:05:09,560
 or by their phone, 'cause they'll tell me all that.

124
00:05:09,560 --> 00:05:12,920
 But for agents, we don't yet have a good way

125
00:05:12,920 --> 00:05:14,640
 of expressing a set of capabilities like that.

126
00:05:14,640 --> 00:05:15,680
 What do they have access to?

127
00:05:15,680 --> 00:05:17,080
 What do they know about me?

128
00:05:17,080 --> 00:05:19,000
 Are they sleep deprived like I am right now?

129
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:20,680
 'Cause I got off a Red Eye last night.

130
00:05:20,680 --> 00:05:23,360
 And you might want to have an identity system

131
00:05:23,360 --> 00:05:25,840
 that supports that kind of richer information.

132
00:05:25,840 --> 00:05:29,960
 - So you talk in your contribution about how Amazon

133
00:05:29,960 --> 00:05:33,840
 uses identifiers, it uses its own sort of spin on SKUs

134
00:05:33,840 --> 00:05:36,800
 to identify products on their site.

135
00:05:36,800 --> 00:05:39,760
 And then in that way to tag their sort of like digital

136
00:05:39,760 --> 00:05:42,080
 representation to the real world.

137
00:05:42,080 --> 00:05:47,080
 What is it about crypto that improves what we already have?

138
00:05:49,120 --> 00:05:51,880
 - Most marketplaces have identity as one of the core

139
00:05:51,880 --> 00:05:55,080
 building blocks of identifying market participants.

140
00:05:55,080 --> 00:05:57,680
 So you go on Amazon and you see these different SKUs.

141
00:05:57,680 --> 00:06:02,680
 Apple gives each app in the App Store, an ID on Facebook.

142
00:06:02,680 --> 00:06:05,560
 You have an identifier that says who you are.

143
00:06:05,560 --> 00:06:07,520
 But they don't expose those very well.

144
00:06:07,520 --> 00:06:08,920
 It's a little hard to pull that out

145
00:06:08,920 --> 00:06:10,640
 and use it in another context.

146
00:06:10,640 --> 00:06:12,920
 We talk about this a lot with Farcaster,

147
00:06:12,920 --> 00:06:15,600
 which is a crypto native social network

148
00:06:15,600 --> 00:06:17,560
 where you can export your identity and use it in other

149
00:06:17,560 --> 00:06:20,880
 places because it's built on blockchain rails.

150
00:06:20,880 --> 00:06:23,680
 And so for agents, one of the beautiful things about them

151
00:06:23,680 --> 00:06:26,280
 is that you can talk to them in multiple channels

152
00:06:26,280 --> 00:06:29,920
 and with multiple contexts, it would be such a shame

153
00:06:29,920 --> 00:06:33,800
 if agents got an identity from just one aggregator

154
00:06:33,800 --> 00:06:36,280
 from one extractive network and then couldn't bring

155
00:06:36,280 --> 00:06:37,600
 that identity to other places.

156
00:06:37,600 --> 00:06:40,200
 'Cause I want to be able to talk to my agent on the phone

157
00:06:40,200 --> 00:06:42,880
 by email in this app and in that app.

158
00:06:42,880 --> 00:06:46,160
 And so I guess, yes, we have any other places

159
00:06:46,160 --> 00:06:49,040
 that we don't have the type of rich identity

160
00:06:49,040 --> 00:06:53,000
 that we need for agents in a way that's available anywhere,

161
00:06:53,000 --> 00:06:54,840
 anytime without the risk of someone being able

162
00:06:54,840 --> 00:06:57,240
 to take it away from the agent.

163
00:06:57,240 --> 00:06:59,280
 I'll add one more thing, which is like,

164
00:06:59,280 --> 00:07:01,520
 the agents, I'm sort of an anthropomorphizing them.

165
00:07:01,520 --> 00:07:03,000
 Really, I'm talking about the entrepreneurs,

166
00:07:03,000 --> 00:07:05,280
 like an entrepreneur builds an agent.

167
00:07:05,280 --> 00:07:07,480
 And this is a new form of an application.

168
00:07:07,480 --> 00:07:10,480
 It's something that you can use and have be useful to you.

169
00:07:10,480 --> 00:07:14,160
 And I don't want entrepreneurs to be beholden

170
00:07:14,160 --> 00:07:17,920
 to a extractive marketplace,

171
00:07:17,920 --> 00:07:20,680
 which is like the only place their agents can live

172
00:07:20,680 --> 00:07:23,800
 when clearly agents deserve to live anywhere.

173
00:07:23,800 --> 00:07:25,400
 That's a core thing to think through,

174
00:07:25,400 --> 00:07:27,120
 is how do we make sure this identity is available

175
00:07:27,120 --> 00:07:29,040
 for anyone to build on top of?

176
00:07:29,040 --> 00:07:30,880
 - So really, what I'm understanding here is,

177
00:07:30,880 --> 00:07:33,800
 it's not just that the agent should have identity.

178
00:07:33,800 --> 00:07:36,080
 It's really this concept of universality

179
00:07:36,080 --> 00:07:37,080
 that is important.

180
00:07:37,080 --> 00:07:38,800
 You want that agent not to just be tied

181
00:07:38,800 --> 00:07:42,600
 to one particular service and potentially aligned

182
00:07:42,600 --> 00:07:45,240
 with their interests over whoever else is.

183
00:07:45,240 --> 00:07:48,400
 You want an agent that is universally identifiable

184
00:07:48,400 --> 00:07:52,040
 across all different services and can be used across them.

185
00:07:52,040 --> 00:07:52,880
 - Yeah, that's right.

186
00:07:52,880 --> 00:07:55,160
 I mean, the universal identity for agents,

187
00:07:55,160 --> 00:07:56,800
 sometimes people call it more like a passport.

188
00:07:56,800 --> 00:07:58,560
 You want the identity to be there,

189
00:07:58,560 --> 00:08:00,360
 but you also want to know a little bit about the agent.

190
00:08:00,360 --> 00:08:02,840
 And maybe you want to be able to stamp

191
00:08:02,840 --> 00:08:04,320
 every time you interact with it.

192
00:08:04,320 --> 00:08:07,040
 So you want to say it's a little bit richer than DNS,

193
00:08:07,040 --> 00:08:08,760
 which is the domain name service,

194
00:08:08,760 --> 00:08:11,760
 which is what underlies URLs and websites,

195
00:08:11,760 --> 00:08:13,760
 because you want it to be stateful

196
00:08:13,760 --> 00:08:16,480
 and you might want to be able to do payments over it.

197
00:08:16,480 --> 00:08:19,880
 But this rich universal identity can be built on blockchains

198
00:08:19,880 --> 00:08:23,560
 and I think likely should be because it guarantees

199
00:08:23,560 --> 00:08:25,840
 that it'll be permissionlessly composable,

200
00:08:25,840 --> 00:08:27,200
 it'll be credibly neutral.

201
00:08:27,200 --> 00:08:28,880
 It'll be accessible from anywhere

202
00:08:28,880 --> 00:08:31,760
 in a way that traditional systems don't guarantee.

203
00:08:31,760 --> 00:08:33,360
 - And by the way, while this is all happening

204
00:08:33,360 --> 00:08:34,280
 in the agent space,

205
00:08:34,280 --> 00:08:38,600
 I think Apple just enabled passports digitally on iPhones.

206
00:08:38,600 --> 00:08:41,040
 So you've got meet space in one direction

207
00:08:41,040 --> 00:08:44,120
 coming online and agents coming in with passports

208
00:08:44,120 --> 00:08:45,320
 on the other side.

209
00:08:45,320 --> 00:08:47,400
 - Yeah, so let's just talk about the first side.

210
00:08:47,400 --> 00:08:51,160
 So Apple is letting you host your passport in Apple.

211
00:08:51,160 --> 00:08:53,000
 And that's like very good.

212
00:08:53,000 --> 00:08:56,440
 But it happens because the government is saying,

213
00:08:56,440 --> 00:08:58,800
 all US citizens can have a passport.

214
00:08:58,800 --> 00:09:01,360
 It's sort of like how Apple is required

215
00:09:01,360 --> 00:09:03,000
 to load websites on the phone.

216
00:09:03,000 --> 00:09:06,480
 Like there is this resource that everyone has

217
00:09:06,480 --> 00:09:09,160
 and can use and Apple is picking it up.

218
00:09:09,160 --> 00:09:11,160
 And I'd like to see a similar thing for agents

219
00:09:11,160 --> 00:09:14,960
 where you have a universal identity

220
00:09:14,960 --> 00:09:17,280
 that people are using to describe agents

221
00:09:17,280 --> 00:09:21,960
 and Apple, Google, meta all refer to that same identity

222
00:09:21,960 --> 00:09:23,800
 instead of them building their own in-house

223
00:09:23,800 --> 00:09:26,680
 and forcing agent developers to do complicated things

224
00:09:26,680 --> 00:09:30,320
 to bridge the agents' characteristics across platforms

225
00:09:30,320 --> 00:09:33,240
 or even worse being stuck within one platform

226
00:09:33,240 --> 00:09:36,000
 and having sort of a toll booth as you try to leave.

227
00:09:36,000 --> 00:09:38,160
 - So you're pointing out that in the Apple context,

228
00:09:38,160 --> 00:09:40,720
 it's really a top-down kind of situation

229
00:09:40,720 --> 00:09:42,240
 and maybe you would be looking more

230
00:09:42,240 --> 00:09:44,120
 for this bottoms-up kind of thing

231
00:09:44,120 --> 00:09:46,520
 where you could have agents get identity

232
00:09:46,520 --> 00:09:48,800
 from all different sorts of places

233
00:09:48,800 --> 00:09:50,280
 and carry that wherever.

234
00:09:50,280 --> 00:09:51,360
 - Yeah, that's right.

235
00:09:51,360 --> 00:09:53,720
 And we're so early that we have the opportunity.

236
00:09:53,720 --> 00:09:54,840
 It's path dependent.

237
00:09:54,840 --> 00:09:57,720
 Like there's a lot of ways agent identity could go.

238
00:09:57,720 --> 00:09:59,280
 And right now because we're at a crossroads,

239
00:09:59,280 --> 00:10:01,680
 I think we should try to make an identity

240
00:10:01,680 --> 00:10:03,120
 that works well for the entrepreneurs

241
00:10:03,120 --> 00:10:04,800
 that are building agents long-term.

242
00:10:04,800 --> 00:10:06,920
 - Okay, well, thanks for joining us, Sam.

